<template>
  <div class="primer-page">
    <section class="hero primer-hero"><div><span class="eyebrow">A HANDS-ON INTRODUCTION</span><h1>Control logic, made visible.</h1><p>CDL describes what a building controller computes. Typed signals enter blocks, connections carry values, and outputs command equipment. Follow the example, change a parameter, and run the building to see the consequence.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="solid-button" href="#lesson-blocks">Start with blocks ↓</a><a class="outline-button" href="#glossary">Block glossary ↗</a></div></div><div class="hero-graphic primer-hero-graph"><PrimerGraph variant="hero" :measured="measured" :setpoint="setpoint" :gain="gain"/><span class="eyebrow primer-hero-label">LIVE VAV BLOCK</span><span class="primer-hero-result">room_temp − cool_setpoint = {{ error.toFixed(1) }} K</span></div></section>
    <nav class="lesson-nav" aria-label="Primer lessons"><a href="#lesson-blocks">01 · Blocks</a><a href="#lesson-connections">02 · Connections</a><a href="#lesson-state">03 · Time & state</a><a href="#lesson-building">04 · Building loop</a><a href="#lesson-points">05 · Brick points</a><a href="#glossary">Glossary ↗</a></nav>

    <section id="lesson-blocks" class="lesson primer-graph-lesson"><div class="lesson-copy"><span class="eyebrow">LESSON 01 / BLOCKS</span><h2>Small functions make a sequence</h2><p>This is the actual cooling-demand branch inside every VAV composite. <code>cool_error</code> subtracts the cooling setpoint from zone temperature. <code>cool_demand_gain</code> scales that error, and <code>cool_demand_limit</code> clamps the diagnostic request to 0–1.</p><div class="lesson-note"><strong>Try it</strong><span>Change any value below. The port values update on the same blocks and wires you see in the visual editor.</span></div></div><div class="primer-graph-controls"><label class="field"><span>Zone air temperature · °C</span><input v-model.number="measured" type="range" min="16" max="30" step="0.1"/><b>{{ measured.toFixed(1) }} °C</b></label><label class="field"><span>Cooling setpoint · °C</span><input v-model.number="setpoint" type="range" min="20" max="28" step="0.1"/><b>{{ setpoint.toFixed(1) }} °C</b></label><label class="field"><span>Demand gain · K⁻¹</span><input v-model.number="gain" type="range" min="0" max="1.5" step="0.05"/><b>{{ gain.toFixed(2) }} K⁻¹</b></label></div><PrimerGraph variant="demand" :measured="measured" :setpoint="setpoint" :gain="gain"/></section>

    <section id="lesson-connections" class="lesson primer-graph-lesson"><div class="lesson-copy"><span class="eyebrow">LESSON 02 / CONNECTIONS</span><h2>Signals follow the wires</h2><p>The same VAV graph branches from <code>cool_error</code> into a Boolean comparison. <code>cooling_mode</code> becomes true only when that error is greater than the 0.5 K deadband. The output then joins the supply-air check to decide whether cooling can open the damper.</p><div class="lesson-note"><strong>Watch for</strong><span>Each wire connects a declared output to one compatible input. A missing input, two drivers, type mismatch, or a feedback cycle without a UnitDelay makes a graph invalid.</span></div></div><div class="primer-graph-explain"><span class="eyebrow">REAL → BOOLEAN</span><p>Move the zone temperature above and below the setpoint plus 0.5 K. The live Boolean output changes inside the block.</p></div><PrimerGraph variant="mode" :measured="measured" :setpoint="setpoint" :gain="gain"/></section>

    <section id="lesson-state" class="lesson"><div class="lesson-copy"><span class="eyebrow">LESSON 03 / TIME & STATE</span><h2>Some blocks remember earlier steps</h2><p>Constants and gains react immediately. A filter, delay, hysteresis, or PI controller stores state across steps. The runtime evaluates the graph in dependency order every 15 minutes, then advances the building thermal model.</p><div class="lesson-note"><strong>Try it</strong><span>Move the input. A shorter filter time constant follows a change faster.</span></div></div><div class="lesson-demo"><div class="demo-header">FIRST-ORDER FILTER PREVIEW</div><label class="field"><span>New input</span><input v-model.number="filterInput" type="range" min="0" max="100" step="1"/><b>{{ filterInput }}</b></label><label class="field"><span>Time constant · minutes</span><input v-model.number="filterTime" type="range" min="1" max="60" step="1"/><b>{{ filterTime }} min</b></label><div class="filter-bars"><div><span>Input</span><i :style="{width:filterInput+'%'}"/></div><div><span>After 15 minutes</span><i :style="{width:filterOutput+'%'}"/></div></div><p class="muted small">This preview assumes the filter previously held zero. A new runtime filter initializes from its first input.</p></div></section>

    <section id="lesson-building" class="lesson"><div class="lesson-copy"><span class="eyebrow">LESSON 04 / CLOSED LOOP</span><h2>The building responds back</h2><p>Two AHUs each serve five VAV zones. Their fans limit actual airflow. In the conventional sample, each AHU mixes 20% outdoor air with return air; the G36-style sample modulates that fraction using measured mixed-air temperature and an editable CDL PI loop. Each AHU coil heats or cools toward the commanded supply temperature as plant output allows. VAV dampers and hot-water reheat then affect zone temperature. At the next step, controllers receive the new zone and supply temperatures.</p><p>The heat pump serves a selected share of heating and cooling, with boiler and chiller covering the remainder. The model charges energy for heat and cooling actually used. VAV heating and cooling demand outputs are diagnostic; the plant request comes from calculated coil and reheat loads.</p><div class="lesson-note"><strong>Explore it</strong><span>Edit an AHU fan gain or VAV setpoint, run the synthetic 2025 year, and compare comfort and energy with the baseline.</span></div></div><div class="lesson-demo building-stack"><div class="plant-row"><div>CHILLER</div><div>BOILER</div><div>HEAT PUMP</div></div><div class="pipe-row"><span>CHILLED WATER LOOP</span><span>HOT WATER LOOP</span></div><div class="plant-row"><div>AHU 1 <small>Zones 1–5</small></div><div>AHU 2 <small>Zones 6–10</small></div></div><div class="zone-dots"><i v-for="n in 10" :key="n" :title="`Zone ${n}`"/></div><p class="muted small">Airflow and duct pressure are normalized. Equipment capacities and dynamics are simplified.</p></div></section>

    <section id="lesson-points" class="lesson"><div class="lesson-copy"><span class="eyebrow">LESSON 05 / POINT MEANING</span><h2>Brick gives points a shared meaning</h2><p>Brick classifies a measured zone temperature as <code>brick:Zone_Air_Temperature_Sensor</code>, an AHU target as <code>brick:Supply_Air_Temperature_Setpoint</code>, and a VAV output as <code>brick:Damper_Position_Command</code>. Teal and lime point cards identify simulation inputs and outputs in the primer and editor. The darker CDL blocks show calculations; every wire uses orthogonal routing.</p><div class="lesson-note"><strong>Look it up</strong><span>The <a href="#glossary">glossary</a> defines every supported block type. Block tooltips show the point class where one applies.</span></div></div><div class="lesson-demo"><div class="demo-header">POINT ROLES</div><div class="signal-path"><div><small>MEASURED</small><strong>Zone air temperature</strong><span>brick:Sensor · °C</span></div><i>→</i><div><small>CALCULATED</small><strong>Controller logic</strong><span>CDL blocks</span></div><i>→</i><div><small>COMMANDED</small><strong>Damper position</strong><span>brick:Command · 0–1</span></div></div><p class="muted small">Modeled values use the generic <code>brick:Point</code> class. A modeled point does not imply a physical sensor.</p></div></section>
  </div>
</template>

<script setup lang="ts">
import { computed, ref } from 'vue'
import PrimerGraph from './PrimerGraph.vue'
const measured=ref(24)
const setpoint=ref(24)
const gain=ref(0.5)
const error=computed(()=>measured.value-setpoint.value)
const filterInput=ref(72)
const filterTime=ref(20)
const filterOutput=computed(()=>filterInput.value*(1-Math.exp(-15/filterTime.value)))
